{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is ending its experimental program to decriminalize certain drugs, which was set to expire on Sunday. A new law will take its place, making it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. Those caught with illicit substances will have the option to choose between being charged or entering a treatment program.\n\nThe new law aims to make it easier for law enforcement to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas. It also encourages counties to create treatment alternatives to divert people from the justice system and towards addiction services. However, opponents argue that this may create a confusing and unfair system.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day explained that the criteria for deflection is narrow and includes requirements such as no other charges, no warrants, and no violent behavior. If a person meets these requirements, officers will call a county hotline to determine eligibility and connect them to a mobile behavioral health team.\n\n*** \n\nThe new law will make personal-use possession of hard drugs a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. This change is set to take effect soon, as the current experimental program is coming to an end.\n\n*** \n\nThe age-old debate surrounding drug use continues to be a contentious issue in many countries. While some argue that decriminalization is the best approach, others believe that harsher penalties are necessary. As the debate rages on, it's clear that there is no easy solution. \n\n*** \n\nIn a country where shopping for groceries is a daily necessity, it's hard to imagine a world where people are struggling with addiction. The tongue-in-cheek idea of eating a whole pizza by oneself is a common joke, but for those struggling with addiction, 